  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...The remnants of post tropical cyclone Isaias will \nbring gusty downsloping winds off the Chautauqua Ridge and then a \nsoaking rain to the region tonight through Monday morning.\n\nCurrent nighttime microphysics satellite imagery and a mosaic of \nradar imagery across the Ohio Valley, shows the northern edge of the \nremnants of post tropical cyclone Isaias, with the northern edge of \nthe precipitation crossing the NY/PA state line. Due to the \nabundance of dry air in place across the region from exiting surface \nhigh pressure (per the 00Z guidance) is acting to erode the \nmoisture, limiting the entering rain to a few sprinkles. With the \nsurface high exiting, a tight surface pressure gradient across the \nregion this evening with southeast flow, is supporting some gusty \nwinds downwind of the Chautauqua Ridge with gusts of up to 35mph. \nHigh pressure will exit and allow the pressure gradient to relax as \nrainfall arrives in a few hours (around 4AM) which will cause winds \nlessen by 7AM.\n\nAs alluded to previously, the steadier rain will arrive by 8 AM \nacross western New York as synoptic scale forcing overspreads the \nregion today from the post-tropical low, widespread rain will \npersist throughout much of the day. Rainfall amounts will range \nbetween a quarter to a half of an inch today, with the higher \namounts focusing across the southern portions of the region across \nthe Finger Lakes region. Rainfall will begin to taper off from west \nto east overnight into early Monday morning as the upper level \ndisturbance begins to shift east across the Mid-Atlantic.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...The next round of rain showers arrives late Monday \nnight into Tuesday with a cold frontal passage across western NY. \n\nThe next low pressure system will pass east across the Great Lakes \nlate Monday and into Tuesday, pushing a cold front southeast across \nthe region Tuesday. Ahead of the front and aligned with a leading \nshortwave, expect another round of rain showers. A few isolated \nembedded thunderstorms can't be ruled out as mid -level lapse rates \napproach 7 degrees Celsius per kilometer. This being said, storms \nwill likely remain elevated due to poor low-level lapse rates.\n\nThere remains quite a bit of uncertainty with high temperatures \nacross the region Tuesday, mainly tied to the timing of \nprecipitation and the frontal passage. While the NBM shows afternoon \nhighs now in the mid to upper 70s across western New  York, the 25th \nand 75th percentile values from input guidance provides a range \nbetween the low 70s to upper 70s for much of western NY and Genesee \nValley.",
